I have enough grounded prior-art to render an auditable novelty verdict. The foundational framework (Mitzenmacher 1902.00732) and LLM output-length-prediction literature (S3 2306.06000; Fu 2408.15792) are confirmed; the unfound IDs were bad guesses, so I rely on those plus the established learning-augmented-scheduling robustness principle.

```
ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "Fu et al., Efficient LLM Scheduling by Learning to Rank, arXiv:2408.15792",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15792",
   why_close: "Verified via fetch — it predicts OUTPUT/decode-length ranks precisely BECAUSE
   absolute length (and by extension cheap input-length signals) is treated as an inadequate
   SJF proxy in LLM serving. The premise 'input-length-SJF is a bad proxy' is the *motivation*
   of this subfield, not a new finding. The claim's headline ('input-length-SJF is bad') is
   near-collision with the assumed prior of this line of work."}
- {cite: "Jin et al., S3: Increasing GPU Utilization during Generative Inference, arXiv:2306.06000",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.06000",
   why_close: "Verified via fetch — builds an output-length predictor for sequence scheduling
   precisely because output length is unknown a priori; same subfield premise that decode length,
   not input length, is the scheduling-relevant quantity."}
- {cite: "Mitzenmacher, Scheduling with Predictions and the Price of Misprediction, arXiv:1902.00732",
   url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00732",
   why_close: "Foundational 'price of misprediction' framework. Packet correctly concedes it (and
   Dell'Amico 1907.04824, SOAP/Gittins 1712.00790) assumes ~unbiased error. The claim's regime
   (biased/anti-correlated proxy) is an EXTENSION of this exact framework, not an orthogonal idea."}
- {cite: "Purohit, Svitkina, Kumar, Improving Online Algorithms via ML Predictions, NeurIPS 2018;
   Lykouris & Vassilvitskii, Competitive Caching with ML Advice, ICML 2018",
   url: "https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2018/hash/73a427badebe0e32caa2e1fc7530b7f3-Abstract.html",
   why_close: "Establishes the consistency/robustness principle: naively trusting BAD predictions
   underperforms the prediction-free baseline, which is *why* robust schedulers fall back to it.
   'Bad-enough predictor => worse than FCFS' is a KNOWN qualitative regime of this theory. NOT
   cited in the packet — must be."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM:
Mixed, leaning thin. The underlying MECHANISM — an anti-correlated/biased size proxy makes
SJF worse than the prediction-free baseline (FCFS) — is a KNOWN regime of learning-augmented
scheduling (Purohit/Lykouris robustness; Mitzenmacher price-of-misprediction), not a new
mechanism. What is genuinely *new-by-quantification*, not renaming: (1) the rank-correlation
SIGN-FLIP crossover tau* with measured magnitudes (SJF/FCFS p99 ratio 1.4-5.8x in the tau~0.14-0.34
band, growing with rho) showing SJF is ACTIVELY HARMFUL, not merely suboptimal — I found NO source
that explicitly states input-length-SJF < FCFS with a tau* crossover; and (2) the ECOLOGICAL VLM
hypothesis that image-token-dominated input sits below tau*. So it is NOT pure renaming, but the
mechanism delta over cited+uncited robustness theory is modest. The oracle control C1 (correct
proxy beats FCFS, ratio 0.72-0.96) is a real and valuable distinction from textbook SJF-starvation,
but it does not by itself make the *novelty* large.

FATAL_COLLISIONS:
None verified to KILL standard. No source (let alone >=2 independent) was found that already
reports input-length-SJF strictly-worse-than-FCFS with a tau* sign-flip crossover, so the
specific quantified phenomenon is not a confirmed duplicate. The collisions above are
NOVELTY-DILUTION, not duplicate-result fatalities. Cannot justify red on collision grounds.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- Purohit/Svitkina/Kumar (NeurIPS 2018) + Lykouris/Vassilvitskii (ICML 2018) — consistency/
  robustness: bad predictions worse than prediction-free baseline (the closest conceptual
  precedent for "below tau*, do FCFS"). Currently UNCITED — required.
- Fu et al. 2408.15792 and S3 (Jin et al. 2306.06000) must be cited specifically as the works
  that ALREADY treat input length as an inadequate SJF proxy, so the contribution is positioned
  as "actively harmful + crossover + VLM ecology," NOT as "input length is a bad proxy" (which
  is not novel).
- Zheng et al., Response Length Perception and Sequence Scheduling (output-length prediction
  for LLM batching) — same input-length-insufficiency premise.
- A real VLM/multimodal-serving scheduling baseline (vLLM/SGLang VLM): I could not confirm an
  existing one via search (web-search tool denied; arXiv fetch only) — L1 must do an explicit
  prior-art sweep to rule out a VLM-specific collision before promotion.

RATIONALE:
The novelty is real-but-thin and load-bearingly GATED on L1. Two grounded facts cap it: (1) the
mechanism is a known regime of learning-augmented scheduling robustness (Purohit/Lykouris;
Mitzenmacher 1902.00732, fetched), and the packet's "foundational works assume unbiased error"
defense is true but only makes this an extension, not a new mechanism; (2) "input-length-SJF is a
bad proxy" is the assumed motivation of the output-length-prediction subfield (Fu 2408.15792 and
S3 2306.06000, both fetched and confirmed). The ONLY genuinely-new pieces — the actively-harmful
tau* crossover quantification and the VLM image-token-below-tau* ecological claim — are, at L0,
respectively (a) a known regime made quantitative in simulation, and (b) an UNMEASURED hypothesis
(tau is imposed via Gaussian copula, not measured on real VLM traces). If the L1 real-trace
Kendall-tau(input_tokens, decode_len) does NOT come in below the simulated tau*, the one piece
that distinguishes this from "expected robustness theory" collapses. No verified >=2-source fatal
collision => not red. Mechanism incremental over cited+uncited robustness theory and motivation
already-assumed in LLM-scheduling subfield => not green. YELLOW, promotion conditional on the L1
real-trace tau measurement plus citing the robustness/output-length-prediction prior art above.
```
